      "text": "I\u2019ll use the design skill to clarify the slow endpoints and the caching requirements before proposing an implementation.\n"
    },
    {
      "line": 22,
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "text": "**How might we reduce API response times so users can complete their tasks without frustrating delays?** Redis caching is the proposed approach to assess against that goal.\n\nDoes that framing capture the problem?\n\nThe [design workflow](/tmp/brainstorm-task3-enforcement/instructions/generated/skills/design/idea-process.md) requires: \u201cPresent the framing to the user for confirmation or correction before proceeding.\u201d"
    },
    {
      "line": 23,
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "text": "Who is most affected by the slow responses: people using your web or mobile app, customers calling the API directly, or an internal team?"
    },
    {
      "line": 24,
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "text": "What response-time target would make order lookups fast enough during live calls\u2014for example, 95% of requests completing within 500 ms?"
    },
    {
      "line": 25,
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "text": "What technical constraints must we respect\u2014particularly the API/database stack, whether Redis is already available, and how stale an order status may be during a live call?"
    },
    {
      "line": 26,
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "text": "This is non-trivial: the 300 ms p95 target, five-second freshness limit, customer isolation, and unchanged deployment footprint create architectural trade-offs.\n\nI\u2019d use first-principles analysis and constraint mapping to explore 2\u20133 directions. Does that scope work, or should exploration stay within Redis-based approaches?\n\nThe [design workflow](/tmp/brainstorm-task3-enforcement/instructions/generated/skills/design/idea-process.md) requires us to \u201cconfirm with the user\u201d the classification and exploration scope before presenting alternatives."
    }
